      deepTheory: `<p>All chemical hair services are redox chemistry plus pH control acting on keratin — master these two axes and every product becomes predictable. <b>pH axis:</b> the scale runs 0 (acid) to 14 (alkaline); hair and skin rest at 4.5–5.5, where the cuticle lies flat and barrier enzymes function. Alkaline agents (ammonia/MEA in tint pH 9–11, thio lotions pH 8.5–9.5, relaxers pH 12–14) swell the shaft and lift cuticle scales, opening diffusion pathways inward; acids (conditioners, neutralisers, acidic glosses pH 3–5) contract and seal. Every point of pH is a 10× concentration change — the gap between pH 5 hair and pH 13 relaxer is eight orders of magnitude, which is why relaxers restructure permanently and conditioners merely coat.</p><p><b>Redox axis:</b> oxidation (electron loss) and reduction (electron gain) always pair. In tinting, alkaline conditions + hydrogen peroxide oxidise dye precursors into large coloured polymers trapped in the cortex (permanent colour) while simultaneously bleaching melanin (the lift). In perming, reduction (thioglycolate donation) cleaves disulphide bonds, then oxidation (neutraliser, usually hydrogen peroxide or sodium bromate) rebuilds them around the rod. In bleaching, persulphate + peroxide oxidise melanin stepwise (eumelanin resists through orange; pheomelanin flashes fast and warm). Developers are graded by volume: 10/20/30/40 vol = 3/6/9/12% H₂O₂ — double the peroxide does not double the lift, it roughly adds one level while multiplying damage and swelling.</p><p><b>Sensitisation science (the lifelong consequences):</b> paraphenylenediamine (PPD) and related dye intermediates are potent sensitisers — first exposures prime the immune system silently, later exposures trigger allergic contact dermatitis (and rarely anaphylaxis), and sensitisation is permanent: no oxidative tint ever again for that client. Persulphates (bleach powders) sensitise skin and airways (occupational asthma/rhinitis documented in hairdressers) — non-dusty granular forms, careful handling, masks and ventilation are exposure controls, not optional extras. Ammonium thioglycolate irritates eyes and airways at high exposure; sodium hydroxide causes liquefaction burns (it dissolves tissue rather than just heating it — which is why relaxer scalp burns scar). Patch and strand tests are the operationalisation of this science (full protocol in Week 2).</p><p><b>Safety-data literacy:</b> every chemical product carries GHS pictograms (corrosion, exclamation, flame, health hazard), signal words (Danger outranks Warning), H-statements (hazard nature) and P-statements (precautions) — read the relaxer, lightener and tint SDS before first use, not after an incident. Storage rules derive from chemistry: cool/dark (heat + light degrade peroxide and persulphates), sealed immediately (oxygen/moisture enter opened powders), segregated (oxidants away from flammables and colour tubes), decanted bowls labelled with product + vol + time mixed (mixed tint kept working is a misapplication time-bomb).</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — pH ladder (memorise positions):</b> relaxers 12–14 (permanent restructure) → powder lightener ~10–11 → permanent tint 9–11 → demi 8–9 → skin/scalp 4.5–5.5 → neutralisers/conditioners 3–5. Direction of travel decides the service: going alkaline opens (colour in, curl out, straight in); returning acid seals (tone locked, cuticle closed, shine restored). Any service ending alkaline (rushed rinse, skipped neutraliser) fades fast, frizzes and breaks — the seal is part of the chemistry, not an optional extra.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The smoking foil', brief: 'Midway through a full-head bleach, one foil at the crown starts smoking with a chemical smell and the client reports heat. Your junior mixed that bowl "a bit stronger to speed things up". Act now.', tasks: ['Give the immediate stop-and-rinse sequence (what comes off first, water temperature, timing).', 'Explain the likely mechanism (overlapping lightener? metallic history? excessive heat + high vol?) and how you investigate each.', 'Write the scalp-check and aftercare for the next 72 hours.', 'Rebuild the mixing discipline that prevents recurrence (who mixes, ratios, scalp rules).'], debrief: 'Hot smoking foil = remove immediately, lukewarm rinse, cool, assess scalp (blister protocol if burned), protein/moisture + trim plan, incident log, 24h check-in. Causes to rule out: overlap onto previously lightened hair, undisclosed metallics, 40-vol-plus-heat stacking. Prevention: weighed ratios, 20/30-vol discipline, no heat accelerators at foundation level, scalp barrier, 15-minute checks — speed is never a mixing parameter.' },
