      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Perming rearranges the hair's permanent architecture: disulphide bonds are reduced (broken), the fibre is reshaped on rods, then bonds are oxidised (rebuilt) in the new configuration. <b>Reduction chemistry:</b> ammonium thioglycolate (alkaline, pH 8.5–9.5) swells the shaft and cleaves S–S bridges efficiently — the workhorse for normal/resistant hair; glyceryl monothioglycolate (acid, pH 6.5–7.5, heat-activated) cleaves gently — the choice for tinted/delicate hair; cysteamine/cysteine systems (low odour, mild) suit mildly textured refreshing; sodium hydroxide relaxers (pH 12–14) don't perm at all — they permanently cleave (lanthionise) bonds for straightening, a different chemistry covered in Week 5. Stronger solution + longer time + smaller rods + more tension = tighter, longer-lasting curl — and more damage; every variable is a trade.</p><p><b>Solution selection matrix:</b> virgin, coarse, resistant → alkaline thio, standard timing; normal/medium → alkaline or exothermic modern systems; tinted/porous → acid or exothermic, reduced time, test-curl driven; bleached/fragile/high-porosity → extreme caution or decline (disulphide already depleted — further reduction dissolves the fibre); previously relaxed/straightened → incompatible with thio perming (overlapping chemistries destroy elasticity — grow out or cut). Water quality matters: hard-water minerals + perm chemistry = unpredictable uptake; chelating pre-wash on suspect water.</p><p><b>Hardware grammar:</b> rod diameter sets curl size (small = tight, large = loose wave — with hair length factored: long hair needs larger rods for the same visual curl); end papers prevent fish-hook bent ends (folded envelope style, smooth, extending past ends); section size ≤ rod diameter coverage (overloaded sections process unevenly — outside over-processed, inside under); base placement (on-base = maximum volume at scalp, half-off = medium, off-base = minimal — directional volume control); tension even and moderate (too tight = breakage + discomfort + weak curl from stretched bonds; too loose = slack undefined curl). Cotton + barrier cream protect hairline/ears; dripping lotion on skin causes irritation — blot, don't wipe.</p><p><b>Processing control:</b> timer from first completed rod (application order = processing order — work efficiently and consistently); test curl at three-quarter time by unwinding one representative rod (firm S-pattern holding its shape = ready; limp = more time; frizzy/fishy smell + mush = over-processed, rinse now and reassess); rinse rods-in with lukewarm water 5+ minutes (residual lotion left behind keeps reducing during neutralising = weak curl + damage); blot thoroughly; neutralise in two applications (5 + 5 minutes typical — first oxidises, second locks, per brand); remove rods gently; rinse and condition with acidic sealer. Document lotion type/strength, rod map, times and test-curl readings — perm memory for rebooks lives on the card.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — perm troubleshooting pairs:</b> limp curl → under-processed (extend time), under-neutralised (full 5+5), rods too large, or lotion too weak for resistance; frizzy weak curl → over-processed, wrong solution strength, or fragile hair accepted; uneven curl → inconsistent section size/tension or application order drift; scalp irritation → dripping lotion, no barrier, too-alkaline solution for sensitivity; rapid drop within weeks → neutraliser short-timed or residual lotion left in. Every failure has a variable — find it on the card, not by guessing stronger next time.</p>`,
