      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Pattern work turns wrapping into design: rod maps are haircut plans drawn in cylinders. <b>Bricklay (staggered bases):</b> offset each row half a rod so section lines never stack — the natural-fall pattern for all-over perms; consistent rod size per zone (or graded larger toward the face for softness). <b>Spiral (vertical winding):</b> long hair wound from ends spiralling down vertical rods — defined, elongated curls; even spiral pitch or the curl tightens/loosens down the length; piggyback/double-rod for short-to-long transitions (short rod at scalp, long rod piggybacked carrying the lengths). <b>Directional design:</b> wind toward the face for framing softness, away for openness; crown whorls get radial placement or they fight the pattern for months.</p><p><b>Tension and paper mastery:</b> end papers folded in envelope style (single-fold for control, double/bookend for fragile ends), extending 3–5 mm past the hair ends so tips never bend (fish-hooks are permanent kinks until cut); winding smooth with the hair ribbon flat — twisted ribbons process into ropey uneven curls; band tension firm enough to hold, light enough to slide one finger under (circulation + comfort + even processing). Scalp comfort check at the front hairline rods — pain now means traction breakage later.</p><p><b>Processing choreography for full heads:</b> apply efficiently nape→crown→sides→front (or per brand order), timer from the first rod, lotion kept off skin with cotton + barrier, test curls from two zones (nape processes differently from heat-exposed crown — crown often ready first). Rinse rods-in thoroughly (5+ min, lukewarm, all zones — residual lotion hides in dense crowns), blot (squeeze, never rub — friction on reduced hair shreds), neutralise 5 + 5 with even saturation (missed rods drop first — the classic 'top layer fell out' complaint), remove rods without pulling, final rinse + acidic condition.</p><p><b>Aftercare that holds the curl:</b> no shampoo 48h (bonds settle), sulphate-free thereafter, wide-tooth combing on conditioned hair only, scrunch-dry/diffuse (brushing dry perm = frizz), protein/moisture rotation by stretch test, heat kept low with protectant, trim cycle 8–12 weeks (ends thin first on permed hair). Document the rod map sketch on the card — re-perms reproduce maps, and maps are intellectual property of the client record.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — pattern chooser:</b> short uniform texture → bricklay, small-medium rods, on-base; long defined curls → spiral vertical, even pitch; volume at roots → on-base placement + smaller rods at crown; soft grow-out → larger rods + off-base at hairline; resistant hair → smaller rod than the target visual (curl relaxes ~20%); fragile/tinted → larger rod, acid/exothermic, reduced time. Sketch every map; photograph every result — pattern libraries compound across a career.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The dropped crown', brief: 'A spiral perm holds beautifully at the nape but the crown dropped within 10 days. Records: single neutraliser pass, "rinsed quickly", no rod map. Diagnose the zonal failure.', tasks: ['Explain why crowns fail first (heat, density, rinse shadows, neutraliser misses).', 'List the protocol breaches and the corrected specification for each.', 'Write the client recovery: options, pricing, and the re-service map.', 'Define the documentation standard that makes zonal failures traceable.'], debrief: 'Crown = hottest, densest, hardest-to-rinse zone: quick rinse left residual lotion, single neutralise missed rods, no map hides it all. Recovery: complimentary zonal re-wrap or full re-service with 5+5 protocol, honest cause statement. Standard going forward: two-zone test curls, timed 5-min rinse, saturated 5+5, sketched map — zonal failures become impossible to hide, hence impossible to repeat.' },
