      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Colour categories are a permanence-and-commitment ladder — prescribing the wrong rung creates corrections. <b>Temporary</b> (coated pigments, shampoos, sprays, chalks — 1 wash; event colour, no chemistry, no damage, no grey coverage); <b>semi-permanent</b> (direct dyes staining the cuticle, 4–8 washes, no developer, no lift — gloss refreshes, fashion tones on pre-lightened hair, gentle grey blending up to ~30%); <b>demi-permanent</b> (low-volume oxidative 5–10 vol, deposits + blends grey to ~50%, minimal lift — the retouch-line eraser, ends refresher, and shine service; acidic demis seal while they deposit); <b>permanent</b> (full oxidative lift + deposit with 10–40 vol — 100% grey coverage with N series, maximum commitment, regrowth maintenance mandatory); <b>lighteners/bleach</b> (persulphate + alkaline cream/powder, lift only, zero tone deposited — always followed by toner/gloss, always with bond care at foundation standard).</p><p><b>Developer ladder with lift math:</b> 10 vol/3% deposits and darkens (gloss, going darker, refreshing — minimal cuticle disturbance); 20 vol/6% lifts 1–2 levels and is the grey-coverage standard (opens resistant cuticle without unnecessary damage); 30 vol/9% lifts 2–3 on healthy hair (blonding, high-lift work); 40 vol/12% maximum lift with maximum swelling and damage (foundation-level use only under direct supervision, never on compromised hair or scalp). Mix ratios alter effective chemistry: 1:1 (standard tint, balanced deposit/lift), 1:1.5–1:2 (high-lift and many blonding lines — more alkalinity, more lift, more swelling). Weigh everything on gram scales; volumetric glugs vary 20%+ between squeezes.</p><p><b>Grey-coverage formulation (worked ladder):</b> under 30% grey — fashion shade + 10–20 vol usually suffices; 30–70% — ½ N + ½ fashion with 20 vol; 70–100% resistant — N-dominant (¾ N + ¼ fashion) or straight N at target level, 20 vol, 0.5 cm sections, full 35–45 min, resistant hairline first. White non-pigmented hair: fashion tones slide translucent without N ballast (the 'pink fade' complaint). Coarse resistant grey: dedicated resistant-grey series or pre-softening per brand. Redheads going grey need N + copper/gold (.3/.4) ballast or flat results.</p><p><b>Formula documentation standard:</b> brand + line + shade(s) with parts + developer vol + ratio + total grams + grey % + natural level + processing time + additives (bond builder, drops) + result photo. This card is a legal record (allergy/reaction traceability), a business record (exact rebook costing), and a professional record (any stylist continues seamlessly). No card = no rebook discipline = corrections within months.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — category chooser (client language):</b> 'try it for the weekend' → temporary; 'refresh my tone, no commitment' → semi; 'blend my sparkle, add shine' → demi; 'cover grey properly / go lighter permanently' → permanent; 'go blonde from dark' → lightener + toner course. Match the chemistry to the commitment the client will actually maintain — permanent colour on a twice-a-year client is a bands guarantee.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The twice-a-year blonde', brief: 'A client wanting permanent full-head blonde admits she visits twice yearly and washes with supermarket clarifying shampoo. Prescribe honestly.', tasks: ['Explain why permanent blonde + biannual visits + clarifying shampoo guarantees bands, breakage and brass.', 'Design the honest alternative (lived-in balayage? demi? lower maintenance level?) with reasoning.', 'Write the home-care non-negotiables with quantities and frequencies.', 'Set the maintenance schedule she must accept, or decline the service.'], debrief: 'Permanent high-maintenance blonde on low-maintenance habits fails by physics. Honest alternatives: lived-in dimension with feathered roots (2–3 visits/year viable), or demi-based blending. Non-negotiables: sulphate-free, purple 1×/week, heat protectant, gloss refreshes. If she rejects maintenance, decline the blonde — integrity over income.' },
