      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Lightening is controlled melanin destruction — the most damaging standard service, governed by staging discipline. <b>Undercoat map (memorise the sequence):</b> levels 1–4 expose red → 5–6 orange → 7–8 yellow-orange → 8–9 yellow → 9–10 pale yellow (tonable). Eumelanin-heavy dark hair lingers in red/orange (more stages); pheomelanin flashes warm fast. Each stage needs enough lightener strength + time + intact elasticity to continue — pushing past the fibre's limit trades hair for levels, and broken hair holds no tone.</p><p><b>Lightener systems:</b> powder/persulphate (fastest, hottest, most swelling — off-scalp foils/balayage; on-scalp only where specifically formulated and supervised); cream (controlled, slower, on-scalp and balayage friendly); oil/gel (gentlest, modest lift, fragile-hair blending). Mix fresh per application (potency decays from mixing — a 30-minute-old bowl is a different product); 20 vol controlled + patience beats 40 vol + fry in every integrity metric; bond additive (Olaplex-style No.1 or equivalent) in the bowl is foundation standard, not luxury. Scalp protocol: barrier cream, 6–13 mm off-scalp default, no overlapping previously lightened hair ever, no heat accelerators at foundation level.</p><p><b>Monitoring cadence:</b> check every 10–15 minutes — wipe a foil edge or sweep a balayage section, read undercoat against the map, pull-test elasticity, assess scalp comfort. Document stage + time per check; stages across visits get photographed so visit 2 starts from evidence. Stop criteria (rinse now, no debate): gummy stretch, smoking/heating beyond warm, scalp pain/burning, white spots (over-processed patches that will snap). Post-lift: lukewarm thorough rinse, bond treatment per brand timing, protein/moisture assessment, then tone only on a sufficiently pale canvas.</p><p><b>Staging across visits (the platinum journey):</b> box-black or level 2–3 to platinum typically needs 2–4 sessions spaced 4–8 weeks with bond care + trims between; each session lifts 2–4 levels safely. Quote the course (sessions + bond treatments + home-care + total), photograph every stage, consent each visit separately. Clients who understand the journey rebook it; clients promised one-day miracles become corrections elsewhere.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — lift decision table:</b> virgin level 6 → level 9 in one session often viable (3 levels, healthy hair, 20–30 vol, bond care); box-dyed level 3 → platinum needs 3+ sessions (bands, unpredictable lift, test panels mandatory); previously lightened fragile lengths → no re-lightening, tone/gloss only; gummy anywhere → stop all lifting, rehabilitate, retest. When the map and the fibre disagree, the fibre wins — always.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The one-day platinum demand', brief: 'Level-3 box-dyed client demands platinum in a single 3-hour appointment for a Saturday wedding, offering double pay. Her elasticity is medium. Respond professionally.', tasks: ['Calculate the honest requirement (levels needed, stages, sessions, spacing) and state what 3 hours can actually achieve.', 'Explain the breakage mathematics of forcing 7 levels in one sitting.', 'Write the refusal-plus-alternative: what you CAN deliver by Saturday safely.', 'Price the full platinum course she actually needs.'], debrief: 'Seven levels in 3 hours on box dye = breakage certainty; double pay does not change chemistry. Deliverable Saturday: gentle test panels + gloss + style (honest glow, intact hair). Course: 3 sessions over 10–16 weeks with bond care, quoted in full. Professionals decline impossible physics kindly — and keep the client for the real journey.' },
