      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Dimensional colour is placement design plus saturation control — the most artistic and most technical colour work. <b>Foil systems in depth:</b> fine weave (alternating thin strands left out — soft, blended, office-friendly); chunky weave (bolder ribbon visibility); slice (solid panels — maximum impact, money-pieces, dimensional panels); back-to-back (maximum blonding density); skipped/sliced spacing (lived-in lightness with depth between); bricklay stagger (rows offset half a foil — no stacked lines, the seamless grow-out); baby-lights (micro-weaves at the hairline/parting — delicate brightness where the eye rests). Saturation edge-to-edge with a feathered root melt (tap, don't push — lightener swell touches scalp and burns); seal firmly (air gaps stall and leak); process by zones (hairline processes fastest from heat — sequence accordingly).</p><p><b>Freehand systems:</b> classic balayage (swept V/W on a board, feathered 2–3 cm transition, open-air slower/softer or filmed brighter); teasylights/foilyage (backcomb, paint the drops, foil for lift — diffused roots with brightness, the premium grow-out); foilage (balayage placement in foils — control + softness); colour melting (demi root smudge melted into mid-lengths — erases harsh foil lines, the correction and grow-out tool); money-piece (face-frame brightness, sliced or weaved, toned brighter than the interior); root shadow/tap (demi depth at the root over foils — depth + blend + extended grow-out). Grow-out design: feathered transitions buy 8–12 weeks; solid lines buy week-4 complaints.</p><p><b>Corrective protocol (the advanced discipline):</b> diagnose in this order — bands (overlap/timing history), brass (under-lift vs faded toner), porosity map (grabby ends, resistant roots), elasticity (can it take more?). Strand-test the correction before promising. Fill science: bleached/level-9+ hair going darker needs missing warm undercoat replaced first (copper/gold filler at the target depth) or the result goes muddy/green and fades hollow — fill, then target shade, then seal. Neutralise only what remains after correct lift/fill (toner is a refiner, not a fixer). Bond care throughout, trim the compromised, photograph every stage.</p><p><b>Consultation and pricing for dimension:</b> reference photos analysed for technique (not just tone — 'this is foilyage with a root tap, 3 hours, this maintenance'); hair history audited for bands/box dye (test panels for every corrective); maintenance contract stated (gloss 4–6 weeks, purple rotation, heat discipline, trim cycle); corrective priced by stages + tests + bond care, never flat-quoted (unknown hair cannot have a known price). Document placement maps — dimensional work is reproducible art only with maps.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — grow-out honesty scale (tell every client):</b> solid foil lines to scalp: 3–4 weeks; bricklay + root tap: 6–8 weeks; feathered balayage/teasylights: 8–12 weeks; lived-in + melting: 12+ weeks with gloss refreshes. Maintenance cost per year roughly inverts with grow-out length — the premium technique is often the cheapest ownership. Put it in writing on the card.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The green-ash disaster', brief: 'Bleached level-10 client wanted "dark chocolate brown". A junior applied level-5 ash directly. Result: muddy green-grey, hollow, already fading. Correct it.', tasks: ['Explain the missing-fill mechanism (no warm undercoat → ash over void = muddy green).', 'Write the two-step correction: filler formula + target formula with levels, processing, seal.', 'Set expectations: visits needed, fade behaviour, maintenance.', 'Define the supervision rule preventing juniors repeating this (fill-before-dark protocol + sign-off).'], debrief: 'Bleach removed all warmth; ash needs a floor to stand on. Correct: copper/gold filler at depth, then 5N-based chocolate, seal, gloss maintenance. One visit usually suffices when filled properly. Rule: no darkening over pre-lightened hair beyond 2 levels without senior fill sign-off — posted at the colour bar.' },
