      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Toning is the finish carpentry of blonding — precision work on a prepared canvas. <b>Wheel mechanics for correction:</b> blue neutralises orange (levels 6–7 remnants), violet neutralises yellow (levels 8–9), blue-violet neutralises gold (level 8–9), green neutralises red (levels 5–6, rare in blonding), ash series (.1) cool, pearl (.2) brighten cool, beige/natural (.0/.3 balance) warm-neutral. The iron rule: toner refines existing level — it cannot lift. Ash over orange = muddy khaki, not clean blonde; the only fix is further careful lift, not stronger toner.</p><p><b>Toner chemistry and timing:</b> demi-permanent acidic formulas (deposit-only, 5–15 min typical) and permanent toners (with lift potential for stubborn warmth — used judiciously) both process visually: apply to damp, evenly saturated hair (dry spots grab darker — the classic root-glow/ends-mud erratic result comes from uneven porosity + dry application), check every 5 minutes by wiping a strand, rinse at target (toners continue developing while you admire them — decide, then rinse), cool rinse, acidic seal. Porous ends grab: dilute toner or apply to lengths last, or pre-fill porosity with clear gloss. Root area processes faster (heat) — apply mid-lengths first on even canvases, or embrace slightly deeper roots as shadow design.</p><p><b>Glossing as a service category:</b> clear + tinted acidic demi glosses refresh faded lengths, blend retouch lines, add mirror shine by sealing the cuticle, and extend colour life 2–4 weeks — the highest-margin 20-minute service in the salon when sold as a course (gloss every 4–6 weeks between full colours). Shades EQ-style acidic demis process ~20 minutes and fade gracefully on tone (no harsh regrowth), making them the maintenance backbone of every blonde column.</p><p><b>Tone maintenance science:</b> violet/blue shampoos deposit pigment per wash — 1× weekly maintains, daily use over-deposits (dull lavender-grey, dryness from cleansing base); sulphate-free colour-safe washing preserves both tone and lipids; cool-to-lukewarm water (hot water swells and bleeds); heat protectant always (thermal fading is real); UV protection outdoors (sun lifts and warms blonde unpredictely); re-tone booked at 4–6 weeks before fade, not after brass. Over-toned (grey/lavender grab): clarify gently + warm gloss to correct, weaken next formula or shorten time — document the adjustment.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — toner troubleshooting pairs:</b> muddy khaki → base too dark/warm for the toner (lift more, tone less); lavender grab on ends → porous over-absorption (dilute, shorten, pre-fill); root glow (hot roots) → heat + overlap (apply lengths first, feather); fade in 1 week → porous canvas + sulphate washing (fill, seal, home-care reset); brass return at week 3 → normal — schedule the gloss cycle, do not re-bleach tone fade.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The khaki blonde', brief: 'Full-head lightener lifted to level 8 gold-orange. The stylist applied ash toner for the full 20 minutes hoping it would "cool it down". Result: muddy khaki, client in tears. Fix the colour and the understanding.', tasks: ['Explain why ash + orange can never equal clean blonde (refine vs lift).', 'Write the correction: further careful lift with bond care OR pivot to a deeper golden-bronde plan — with decision criteria.', 'Specify the toner, timing and application order for the corrected canvas.', 'Set the maintenance that holds the final result to week 6.'], debrief: 'Khaki = correct diagnosis (warmth) with the wrong tool (toner instead of lift). If elasticity allows: gentle re-lift of orange zones, then blue-violet toner visually timed. If not: pivot to rich bronde with client consent — honesty beats breakage. Either way: gloss cycle, purple rotation, heat discipline to week 6.' },
