      passMark: 0.7,
      deepTheory: `<p>Chemical safety is a rehearsed system, not a poster on the wall. <b>Exposure routes and controls:</b> inhalation (persulphate dust, ammonia vapour, thioglycolate fumes, aldehydes from classic keratin — controls: non-dusty formulas, closed containers, local ventilation/fume extraction at the mixing zone, masks for dusty operations, asthmatics flagged at consultation); skin contact (tint, bleach, relaxer — controls: fitted nitrile gloves changed when torn or every 30–45 min, barrier cream, apron, no bare-hand 'feeling the mix'); eye splash (tint/lightener flicked during application — controls: careful brush discipline, eyewash station or sterile pods within 10 seconds reach, goggles for backwash-heavy blonding); ingestion (never eat/drink/handle contact lenses with gloved hands — the rule is absolute).</p><p><b>First-response protocols (posted at the backwash):</b> eye splash — irrigate with clean lukewarm water or sterile saline 15+ minutes, hold lids open, remove contact lenses if present, seek medical care for anything beyond transient redness, document; skin burn/irritation — remove product immediately with cool water (not neutralising chemicals on skin — dilution, not chemistry), cool compress, cover blisters without bursting, GP/dermatology referral for anything beyond mild transient redness; fume/dust event — fresh air, stop the service, ventilate, monitor asthmatics closely (rescue inhaler per their own plan, emergency services for breathing difficulty); bleach dust spill — mask up, damp-clean (never dry-sweep into airborne cloud), ventilate, dispose as chemical waste per local rules (never wash bulk persulphate down drains — environmental harm + plumbing reactions).</p><p><b>Reaction recognition ladder:</b> mild irritation (transient itch/redness resolving on rinse — document, watch, adjust formula); allergic contact dermatitis (spreading itch, swelling, vesicles hours–days later — GP pathway, no further oxidative chemistry, incident form + photos); respiratory sensitisation (cough/wheeze with bleach work developing over months — occupational health referral, dust-free handling, possible role modification); anaphylaxis signs (lip/tongue swelling, breathing difficulty, dizziness — emergency services, upright, onset/product noted, never administer drugs yourself). PPD reactions can escalate across exposures — a 'mild tingle last time' is a warning, not reassurance.</p><p><b>Governance that survives inspection:</b> chemical inventory list with SDS accessible (paper or terminal, staff trained to find in under a minute); decant/bowl labelling (product + vol + time mixed + initials); incident book with photos and follow-ups; monthly pattern review (same fault twice = system change); PPE stock checks (glove sizes, masks, eyewash expiry); ventilation maintenance (filters, fan function); pregnancy/breastfeeding policy written (defer major corrections, maximise ventilation, document choices). Drills quarterly: spill, splash, burn-report, evacuation — timed and logged like fire drills.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — backwash emergency strip (laminate above the basin):</b> EYES → irrigate 15+ min → medical if beyond transient redness. SKIN burn → cool water now → cover blisters → GP if beyond mild. FUMES → fresh air + stop service → monitor breathing. SPILL → mask + damp-clean + ventilate. ALWAYS → document + photo + 24h follow-up call. Emergency numbers posted beside it. Rehearsed quarterly beats read annually.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The asthmatic bleacher', brief: 'A colourist with developing bleach-dust cough (worse on double-bleach days) hides it to keep her column. You are the senior noticing. Act for her health and the salon liability.', tasks: ['Name the condition pathway (respiratory sensitisation) and why hiding it is dangerous.', 'Specify immediate exposure controls (dust-free formulas, handling, masks, ventilation, task rotation).', 'Write the occupational-health referral conversation with her — supportive, non-punitive, documented.', 'Redesign the rota/product policy so sensitised staff keep working safely.'], debrief: 'Persulphate sensitisation progresses with exposure — early action preserves careers. Controls now: non-dusty lighteners, closed handling, extraction, fit-tested masks, bleach-day rotation. Conversation: health-first, job-secure, documented consent. Policy: sensitisation screening at induction + annually, task modification without pay penalty — healthy teams retain talent.' },
