      deepTheory: `<p>Troubleshooting is diagnosis under pressure: map the fault, prove the fix on a strand, stage the correction, document everything. <b>Fault atlas:</b> bands (overlap/timing history — feathered re-application, no overlap, blend with demi); brass (under-lifted base — further careful lift, never stronger toner; or faded toner — re-tone on sufficient base); green/muddy (missing fill under darkener, or copper/chlorine — fill/correct per cause); fade/hollow (porous canvas + sulphate washing — fill + demi + seal + home-care reset); frizz/breakage (over-processing — protein/moisture triage + cut plan, chemicals paused); perm/relaxer drop (under-neutralised, residual lotion, wrong strength — re-service with full protocol or pivot style); scalp issues (burns, allergy — medical pathway, incident governance).</p><p><b>Correction choreography:</b> (1) strand-test the exact fix (formula, timing, elasticity delta) — corrections promised without strand proof are gambles with the client's hair; (2) stage across visits where integrity demands (banded bleach to even blonde is typically 2–3 sessions); (3) bond care + fill + tone + seal in that order; (4) trim the unsalvageable frankly (nursing dead ends wastes everyone's money); (5) photograph every stage same-light. Pricing: tests + stages + bond care + home-care, quoted before mixing — corrective work at standard rates loses money and corners get cut; price the expertise.</p><p><b>Portfolio as proof-of-work:</b> before/during/after same light/angle/distance, formula cards attached, consent specifying channels, honest captions with techniques named (clients search 'teasylights correction' — tag accurately). Ten-plus documented cases across bands/brass/fade/drop make a junior employable anywhere; corrections are the highest-trust work in the salon and the fastest reputation builder.</p><p><b>Final assessment standard:</b> 30-question theory ≥70% (distinction 85%+) plus a presented chemical case (consult + tests + formula + staged photos + aftercare) and one observed live chemical service, scored against the rubric. Pass means supervised chemical junior — explicitly not a solo bleacher. The sign-off states scope honestly ('assists and retouches under supervision; independent bleaching after X logged corrections'), which is what makes employers trust the certificate and what keeps newly qualified stylists' clients safe.</p>`,
      deepExtra: `<p><b>Reference — correction quote script:</b> 'Your hair needs [stages] because [diagnosis in plain words]. Each visit is [service + price], total [range], home-care [items + price], timeline [weeks]. Tests today are [price, redeemable on booking]. Here are two similar corrections I have documented.' Itemised, photographed, consented — corrections sold this way have near-zero disputes and premium positioning.</p>`,
      caseStudy: { title: 'The full correction client', brief: 'New client: banded box-dye lengths, orange mid-band, gummy ends, wants "even blonde, healthy, one visit, fixed price". Run the whole professional sequence.', tasks: ['Deliver the diagnosis in plain words with the strand-test plan (which zones get tested, what each proves).', 'Design the staged correction: visits, services, formulas logic, bond/trim plan.', 'Quote using the script structure with ranges and redeemable tests.', 'Define your walk-away conditions (which test results cancel the plan).'], debrief: 'Bands + orange + gummy = multi-stage correction or decline. Tests: band zone (lift behaviour), ends (integrity limit), virgin regrowth (baseline). Stages: gentle band work + bond + trim, then progressive blonding, then tone — or pivot to lived-in brunette if integrity fails. Walk-away: gummy test panels, scalp issues, or client rejecting staged pricing. The strand decides; the script sells; the walk-away protects.' },
